\details{
A tidyselect-style API for querying and rewriting either of q2r's
two AST representations: the \code{\link{pandoc}} S7 hierarchy and the
\code{\link{ts_tree}} tree-sitter AST. Each verb is an S7 generic with methods
on the relevant node types; the same verb name works on both ASTs.

Predicates are unquoted R expressions evaluated against each candidate
node with a per-AST data mask. The mask exposes the node's S7 slots
as bare names (\code{level}, \code{url}, \code{text}, \code{kind}, \code{is_named}, ...) plus a
set of helper functions (\code{is()}, \code{has_class()}, \code{has_id()},
\code{has_attr()}, \code{has_text()}, \code{has_label()}, \code{is_leaf()}). Multiple
predicates are combined with \code{&} (logical AND).
}
\section{Scope}{

The verbs walk the document's block tree only. Document metadata
(\verb{@meta}, including fields like \code{title} that parse as inline trees)
and the args of nested shortcodes are not visited - a predicate can
never match inside them, and \code{\link[=ast_text]{ast_text()}} does not include them.
}

\section{Selection verbs}{

\itemize{
\item \code{\link[=select_nodes]{select_nodes()}} descends the whole tree (including the root)
and returns a flat list of matching nodes.
\item \code{\link[=select_descendants]{select_descendants()}} is the same but excludes the root. Accepts
a list of nodes (so pipe chains work).
\item \code{\link[=select_children]{select_children()}} only checks the direct children.
\item \code{\link[=select_first]{select_first()}} returns the first match or \code{NULL}.
}
}

\section{Iteration and mutation verbs}{

\itemize{
\item \code{\link[=walk_nodes]{walk_nodes()}} applies a side-effect function to every match in
pre-order (document order, parent before children) on both ASTs;
returns its input invisibly.
\item \code{\link[=map_nodes]{map_nodes()}} rewrites every match via \code{.f}. The function may
return a single node (in-place replacement), a list of nodes
(spliced in at the match site), \code{NULL} (delete), or the original
node (no-op). Applied to a document / tree / wrapper the result is
the same class as the input; applied to a bare node the result
follows the mutation contract directly (it may be a node, a list, or
\code{NULL}).
\item \code{\link[=replace_nodes]{replace_nodes()}} is \code{map_nodes()} with a constant replacement.
\item \code{\link[=delete_nodes]{delete_nodes()}} is \code{map_nodes()} with \verb{\\(x) NULL}.
\item \code{\link[=splice_nodes]{splice_nodes()}} is \code{map_nodes()} whose \code{.f} must return a list.
\item \code{\link[=insert_before]{insert_before()}} / \code{\link[=insert_after]{insert_after()}} inject siblings around
each match.
}
}

\section{Mutation contract}{

\code{.f} is called with each matching node as its argument. Its return
value is interpreted by the walker:
\itemize{
\item A single node of the appropriate kind replaces the original.
\item A list of nodes is spliced into the parent's child list at the
match's position.
\item \code{NULL} removes the match from the parent.
\item Returning the original node (or an \code{==} equivalent) is a no-op.
}

The mutation walker traverses bottom-up (post-order), so when a
parent is checked its children have already been rewritten. This
matches Pandoc Lua filters' default.

The singular support slots (a figure or table caption, a table's head
and foot) are reachable by the mutation verbs but under a restricted
contract: \code{.f} must return a node of the same class or \code{NULL}, which
resets the slot to an empty instance (the slot itself cannot be
removed or spliced).

On a \code{ts_tree}, the three grammar-gap \emph{content} kinds (\code{pandoc_math},
\code{pandoc_display_math}, \code{code_fence_content}) round-trip through their
verbatim source bytes, so mutating \emph{their} children is a no-op on
\code{\link[=to_qmd]{to_qmd()}} output.

A mutation verb applied to a whole \code{ts_tree} renders and reparses the
result before returning it, so the returned tree always carries
internally consistent byte ranges and chained mutations are safe.
Applied to a bare \code{\link{ts_node}} there is no document to reparse: the
result is reliable for a single mutation pass, but should be
re-rendered (or the chain restructured at the tree level) before
further mutation.
}

\section{Predicate helpers (available inside \code{...})}{

These are installed into the predicate's data mask, so inside a
predicate they take the zero-node forms shown below (testing the
current node). Most also exist as exported node-first functions -
see \code{\link[=node_predicates]{has_id()}} and friends - for use on a node you
already hold.
\itemize{
\item \verb{is(<S7 class>)} honours S7 inheritance, so \code{is(pandoc_block)}
matches any block.
The attribute- and text-based helpers (\code{has_class}, \code{has_id},
\code{has_attr}, \code{has_text}, \code{has_label}) resolve \verb{@attr} / \code{\link[=ast_text]{ast_text()}},
which exist only on the pandoc AST, so on a \code{ts_tree} they are a silent
no-match. Use \code{\link[=ts_query]{ts_query()}} or bare-slot predicates (\code{kind}, \code{text})
for tree-sitter queries.
\item \code{has_class("foo")} / \code{has_class(c("foo", "bar"))} test
\verb{@attr@classes} membership (pandoc only).
\item \code{has_id("intro")} tests \verb{@attr@id} (pandoc only).
\item \code{has_attr("key")} / \code{has_attr("key", "val")} test
\verb{@attr@attributes} (pandoc only).
\item \code{has_text("Exercise")} tests the node's flattened text
(\code{\link[=ast_text]{ast_text()}}) against one or more regex patterns (\code{fixed = TRUE}
for literal matching); the analog of parsermd's \code{has_heading()}
(pandoc only).
\item \code{has_label("fig-*")} glob-matches the node's \verb{@attr@id}, where
Quarto labels surface as \verb{#id}; for code cells without an attr id it
falls back to the cell's \code{label} option. The analog of parsermd's
\code{has_label()} (pandoc only).
\item \code{is_code_cell()} matches an executable Quarto cell (see
\code{\link{code_cell}}).
\item \code{has_option("eval")} / \code{has_option("eval", FALSE)} test a cell's
\verb{#|} options.
\item \code{has_engine("r")} / \code{has_engine(c("r", "python"))} test a cell's
engine (\code{\link[=cell_engine]{cell_engine()}}).
\item \code{is_leaf()} matches nodes with no children.
\item \code{is_named} (a bare slot, tree-sitter only, not a function call)
is the \code{ts_node} named/anonymous flag.
\item \code{starts_with()}, \code{ends_with()}, \code{matches()}, \code{contains()} - string
tests usable as e.g. \code{starts_with("http", url)}.
\item \code{any_of(x)} and \code{all_of(x)} - splice a character vector for use
with \code{\%in\%}.
\item Bare slot access: \code{level}, \code{url}, \code{title}, \code{text}, \code{format},
\code{kind}, \code{class}, \code{quote_type}, \code{math_type}, etc. Missing slots
resolve to \code{NULL} (so \code{NULL == 2} is \code{FALSE}, not an error).
}
